1.Which input type allows a user to select a date (year, month, day)?
A)time
B)calendar
C)date  //answwrs
D)datetime


✅ Correct Answer: C. date

Easy English Explanation

The <input type="date"> lets the user select a date:

📅 Year
📅 Month
📅 Day
Example:
<input type="date">

When you click it, a calendar opens so you can pick a date.

Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. time → Selects only the time (hours and minutes).
❌ B. calendar → Not a valid HTML input type.
❌ D. datetime → Not used in modern HTML5. (datetime-local is used instead.)
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
date = 📅 Date (Year + Month + Day)
time = 🕒 Time only
datetime-local = 📅 + 🕒 Date and Time
One-Line Memory:

type="date" = Choose Year, Month, and Day.

✅ Answer: C. date


2.Which tag is used to create a non-selectable label for a group of options in a dropdown?
A)<group>
B)<optgroup>     //answers
C)<label>
D)<section></section>


✅ Correct Answer: B. <optgroup>

Easy English Explanation

The <optgroup> tag is used to group related options inside a <select> dropdown.

The group label cannot be selected. Only the options inside it can be selected.

Example:
<select>
  <optgroup label="Fruits">
    <option>Apple</option>
    <option>Mango</option>
  </optgroup>

  <optgroup label="Vegetables">
    <option>Carrot</option>
    <option>Potato</option>
  </optgroup>
</select>

Here:

Fruits → Group label (❌ cannot be selected)
Apple, Mango → Options (✅ can be selected)
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. <group> → Not a valid HTML tag.
❌ C. <label> → Used to label form elements, not to group dropdown options.
❌ D. <section> → Used to organize webpage content, not dropdown options.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
<option> = One choice ✅
<optgroup> = Group of choices 📂
One-Line Memory:

<optgroup> = Creates a non-selectable heading for options in a dropdown.

✅ Answer: B. <optgroup></optgroup>


3.Which method appends the form data to the URL in name/value pairs?
A)POST
B)SEND
C)GET  //answeers
D)PUT

PUT

✅ Correct Answer: C. GET

Easy English Explanation

The GET method sends the form data by adding it to the URL as name=value pairs.

Example:
<form action="/search" method="get">
  <input type="text" name="q">
  <input type="submit">
</form>

If the user types HTML, the URL becomes:

/search?q=HTML

Here:

q = name
HTML = value
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. POST → Sends data in the request body, not in the URL.
❌ B. SEND → Not an HTML form method.
❌ D. PUT → Used mainly in APIs, not standard HTML forms.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
GET = Get through URL 🌐
POST = Post inside the body 📦
One-Line Memory:

GET = Data is visible in the URL as name=value.

✅ Answer: C. GET

4.What does the <label> tag's for attribute connect to?
A)The input's name
B)The input's class
C)The input's id //answes
D)The form's action


✅ Correct Answer: C. The input's id

Easy English Explanation

The for attribute in the <label> tag connects to the id of an <input> element.

When you click the label, the input field is automatically selected.

Example:
<label for="username">Username:</label>
<input type="text" id="username">

Here:

for="username" ✅
id="username" ✅

Both values must be the same.

Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. name → Used when sending form data, not for connecting labels.
❌ B. class → Used for CSS and JavaScript.
❌ D. action → Specifies where the form data is sent.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
for → looks FOR the id 👀
for = id (same value)
One-Line Memory:

<label for=""> always connects to the input's id.

✅ Answer: C. The input's id



5.Which attribute in an <option> tag makes it the default choice when the page loads?

A)default
B)active
C)selected  //answer
D)checked


✅ Correct Answer: C. selected

Easy English Explanation

The selected attribute makes an <option> the default choice when the page first loads.

Example:
<select>
  <option>Apple</option>
  <option selected>Mango</option>
  <option>Orange</option>
</select>

When the page opens:

✅ Mango is selected automatically.
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. default → Not a valid attribute for <option>.
❌ B. active → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ D. checked → Used for checkboxes and radio buttons, not <option>.
Easy T
rick to Remember 🧠
selected = Selected by default ✅
checked = Checkbox/Radio ☑️
One-Line Memory:

selected = Default option in a dropdown.

✅ Answer: C. selected


