7.What happens if you add an option with no value attribute?
A)The form crashes
B)The text inside the tag is used as the value //answers
C)It sends a null value
D)It is not selectable


✅ Correct Answer: B. The text inside the tag is used as the value.

Easy English Explanation

If an <option> does not have a value attribute, the browser uses the text inside the <option> as the value.

Example:
<select>
  <option>Apple</option>
  <option value="M">Mango</option>
</select>

If the user selects:

Apple → Value sent = "Apple" ✅ (text is used)
Mango → Value sent = "M" ✅ (because value="M" is given)
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. The form crashes → No, the form works normally.
❌ C. It sends a null value → No, it sends the option text.
❌ D. It is not selectable → No, it can still be selected.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
No value? → Use the visible text.
Has value? → Use the value attribute.
One-Line Memory:

No value attribute = Option text becomes the value.

✅ Answer: B. The text inside the tag is used as the value.


8.Which attribute is used to specify a regular expression for input validation?
A)regex
B)validate
C)pattern  //answers
D)match

✅ Correct Answer: C. pattern

Easy English Explanation

The pattern attribute is used to define a regular expression (regex) for input validation.

It checks whether the user's input matches the required format.

Example:
<input type="text" pattern="[A-Za-z]{3,}">

This means:

✅ Only letters (A–Z, a–z)
✅ At least 3 letters

Examples:

✔️ Ram → Valid
✔️ Gowtham → Valid
❌ R1 → Invalid (contains a number)
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. regex → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ B. validate → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ D. match → Not a valid HTML attribute.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
Pattern = Pattern to follow
pattern → Checks the input format
One-Line Memory:

pattern = Uses a regular expression (regex) to validate user input.

✅ Answer: C. pattern

